This folder contains the tests of Simu5G and the tools used to validate a
change to the model.

 - fingerprint/  Fingerprint tests: every example simulation, run for a short
                 time, against a recorded hash of what it did. A change
                 detector, not an oracle.
 - unit/         Unit tests: classes exercised without a running simulation,
                 including verbatim transcriptions of the 3GPP formulas.
 - module/       Module tests: small simulations checked by their outcome,
                 including the configurations that must be rejected.
 - physics/      Physics tests: small simulations graded against closed-form
                 values computed from the scenario's parameters.
 - abtest/       A replicated A/B comparison of two versions of the code,
                 statistic by statistic, over many seeds.
 - doc/          Records of how larger changes were validated and accepted.

Each subfolder has a README of its own on how to run its tests and how to
write new ones. The statistical baselines (the run-0 scalars of every example
configuration) are kept in a separate git repository, checked out as
statistics/ in the project root.

Validating a model change
-------------------------

No single suite says whether a change is right. Each answers one question,
and the kind of change decides which questions matter.

 - A refactoring must not change behavior in any state. The fingerprint suite
   is the check: every row identical. A change that only moves the module
   tree moves the module-id ingredients (tplx, tilx) and nothing else. When a
   refactoring moves the event structure or the traffic timing (tplx, ~tNl)
   but not the results (sz), compare per-reception traces of the two versions:
   identical inputs and decisions for every reception show that only events
   moved. Remember that the suite covers only what its rows exercise; a
   refactoring is also reviewed for states no row reaches.

 - A change that re-deals the random draws without changing the model -- an
   RNG draw added or removed, state keyed differently -- moves almost every
   fingerprint and baseline, and says nothing by itself. What it must not
   change are the distributions of the results. Run abtest/ on the
   configurations it moves: no statistic may differ beyond what the null
   control (the same code, other seeds) shows.

 - A fix, or an intended change of the model, comes with a test that fails
   before it and passes after it, placed in the history before the fix. The
   A/B comparison then shows what the change does to the results, and that
   nothing else moved; narrow down any unexplained difference to its commit.

 - Every new check must be able to fail. Break the mechanism it guards -- a
   mutant of the code under test, for example the sum of two distances where
   the larger one belongs -- and confirm that the check, and preferably only
   the check, fails.

 - A test against a specification is a verbatim transcription of it. Where
   the model deviates, the deviation is recorded twice: a passing check that
   pins today's behavior, labelled "PINNED DEFECT:", and a separate test with
   %expected-failure that asserts the specification. opp_test reports an
   expected-failure test that starts to pass as a plain PASS, which is why
   the pin is needed; and it reports a test that aborts at startup as the
   same EXPECTEDFAIL, so check that an expected failure fails on its checks
   (its test.out holds modelchecks=N modelfailures=M with M > 0, and no
   error).

 - The statistical baselines record the run-0 results at every move of the
   development branch. They are a record, not an oracle: a commit that only
   re-deals the random draws moves all of them.

doc/ holds the records of changes that were validated this way.
